    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Transient receptor potential (TRP) channels are a family of receptor proteins regulating pain sensation. TRP receptors are activated by a variety of noxious stimuli, such as capsaicin (chili peppers), heat, and mechanical stress. Chronic TRPV1 stimulation leads to desensitization of the receptor, a process which is mediated by calcium/calmodulin dependent kinases. This attribute, however, has yet to been leveraged for treatment of chronic pain diseases. Here, we identify a potential C-terminal phosphorylation site that is involved in regulating level of TRPV1 expression at the cell surface. In dorsal root ganglion cells, confocal microscopy and western blot show that de-phosphorylation of threonine 705 through creation of a T705A mutation is sufficient to induce significantly increased expression at the plasma membrane. Mimicking constitutive phosphorylation through a T705E mutant at that site induces the receptor to be retained on intracellular membranes. TRPV4 - another member of the TRPV family - also has a similar trafficking switch region that contains two adjacent threonines T739 and T740, both potentially involved in regulating TRPV4 surface expression. Confocal and western blot data show that removing the potential T740 phosphorylation site is sufficient to significantly increase surface trafficking. However, the phosphomimetic mutation reduced but did not eliminate surface expression.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
